<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Trianon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1920">1920</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Trianon was the peace treaty signed on 4 June 1920 at the Grand Trianon palace in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> between the Allied powers and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. It cut Hungary's territory by about two-thirds, transferring lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, the Kingdom of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovenes">Slovenes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and left millions of Hungarians outside the new borders. Its revision became the central aim of interwar Hungarian politics.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-trianon.jpg" alt="Treaty of Trianon" width="320" height="242" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Agence Rol · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Signature_de_la_Paix_avec_la_Hongrie,_en_t%C3%AAte_Ben%C3%A1rd_%C3%81gost_hongrois(passant_devant_un_piquet_d%27honneur_%C3%A0_Versailles).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> had been at war since August 1914 as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a>. On 31 October 1918, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aster-revolution">Aster Revolution</a>, King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-iv">Charles IV</a> appointed the liberal Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mihaly-karolyi">Mihály Károlyi</a> prime minister, and Hungary declared its independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Károlyi had the army, which still numbered more than 1,400,000 soldiers, unilaterally disarmed, and an armistice was signed on 13 November 1918. Serbian, Czechoslovak and Romanian forces crossed the borders in the following days, and Hungary's neighbours blockaded the country.</p><p>On 21 March 1919 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bela-kun">Béla Kun</a> led a communist coup and proclaimed the Hungarian Soviet Republic. In June the Entente ordered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> to stop their advance and accept new demarcation lines as future borders. After Kun's fall Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miklos-horthy">Miklós Horthy</a> entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> on 16 November 1919, and Count Albert Apponyi led the Hungarian delegation to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris-peace-conference-1919-1920">Paris Peace Conference</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty, dictated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> rather than negotiated, was signed in the Grand Trianon at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> on 4 June 1920 and came into force on 26 July 1921. It legalised the existing demarcation lines as the new borders. The Allies refused plebiscites, explaining in the Millerand letter that a popular consultation would not produce significantly different results; the letter also suggested that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations">League of Nations</a> might mediate border corrections, which Hungarian diplomats read as a promise.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> became a landlocked state of 93,073 square kilometres and 7.6 million people, and 3.3 million Hungarians lived in the areas allocated to its neighbours. The army was limited to 35,000 men, heavy artillery, tanks and air force were forbidden, and the Austro-Hungarian Navy ceased to exist. Hungary had to pay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> and recognise minority rights. The treaty also ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a> and ordered the resumption of trade and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> supplies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Hungarian delegation signed under protest, and agitation for revision began at once. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irredentism">Irredentism</a>, the demand for reunification of Hungarian peoples, became a central theme of Hungarian politics and diplomacy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanians">Romanians</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a>, and Slovaks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a> helped form <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, but as many as a third of Hungarian speakers found themselves outside the new borders. The economic effects were severe, since the Austro-Hungarian economy had been one unit: mills around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/budapest">Budapest</a> operated at 20% capacity. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>'s boundaries have remained mostly the same since 1920, apart from the short-lived expansion of 1938–1941. The treaty is known in Hungary as the Peace Dictate of Trianon.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Trianon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Treaty of Saint-Germain added Subcarpathian Ruthenia, and the <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> in 1920 set the border with Hungary.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Revision of the <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> dominated Hungary's politics and foreign policy, and the resentment it caused has lasted to the present.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-hungary-1920-1946">Kingdom of Hungary (1920-1946) · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>At the end of the First World War assemblies in Transylvania, Bessarabia and Bukovina voted for union with Romania, and the Treaty of Saint-Germain, the <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> and the Treaty of Paris of 1920 assigned most of these lands to it.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-romania">Kingdom of Romania · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The borders were settled in the peace treaties of Saint-Germain, Neuilly and <b>Trianon</b> and, with Italy, in the Treaty of Rapallo of 1920, while Carinthia voted to stay with Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The peace treaties of Saint-Germain and <b>Trianon</b> assigned German-speaking western Hungary, later called Burgenland, to Austria.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-leitha">Banate of Leitha</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Treaty of Trianon</b> in 1920 transferred two-thirds of the former kingdom to its neighbours.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-hungary-1867-1918">Kingdom of Hungary (1867-1918)</a></li></ul></section>
